WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.494
EDR telemetry is the activity data collected from endpoints.

00:00:03.494 --> 00:00:06.889
Common examples include process starts, command-line arguments, file writes,

00:00:06.889 --> 00:00:10.660
network connections, user logins, privilege changes, script activity, service creation,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.546
scheduled tasks, and registry modifications.

00:00:12.546 --> 00:00:15.940
A single event may not mean much by itself.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.826
The value comes from context.

00:00:17.826 --> 00:00:23.483
A command-line tool might be normal for an administrator but suspicious on a receptionist's laptop.

00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:28.386
A network connection may be normal for one application but strange for another.

00:00:28.386 --> 00:00:34.420
EDR helps analysts connect events into a timeline so they can understand whether activity is benign,

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:35.551
suspicious, or malicious.

00:00:35.551 --> 00:00:40.077
That timeline can show what started first, what process launched another process,

00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:44.980
which user was involved, what network destination was contacted, and what files changed.
